  "id": "good-imine-formation-from-acetaldehyde-and-methylamine",
  "title": "Methylamine adds to acetaldehyde, a proton moves from nitrogen to oxygen, acid protonates the hydroxyl, water leaves to the iminium, and water takes the last proton off nitrogen",
  "expect": {
    "kind": "good",
    "note": "Five steps, four of which move a proton, and the longest pathway in the corpus. It is here because it is the mechanism where students most often skip the proton transfers and jump from the carbinolamine to the imine in one arrow. Step 2 is the one that is easiest to lose and hardest to justify losing: the nitrogen is positive and the oxygen is negative in the addition product, and nothing further happens until that is fixed. It is drawn as an intramolecular transfer within one species, which conservation-proton-transfer still tracks atom by atom and still balances. Step 4 is the other load bearing one: the nitrogen lone pair pushes in AS water leaves, so no carbocation is formed and the intermediate is an iminium with a full octet."
